Where Do Liberal Economies, the Rule of Law, and Democracy
Stand Today?
Liberal Economies
Liberal economies remain a dominant global
force, with free-market principles continuing to
drive growth in many regions. The global trade
system, anchored by institutions like the World
Trade Organization (WTO) and supported by
agreements such as the European Single
Market and the North American trade
arrangements, has promoted economic
interdependence and efficiency.
Major economies such as the United States,
the European Union, and Japan still rely on
free-market capitalism, fostering innovation
and global competition. Further, in Asia, liberal
economic principles helped lift over 1.2 billion
people out of extreme poverty from 1990 to
2015, according to World Bank data, largely
due to growth driven by global trade and
foreign investment.
However, the liberal economic model has
come under increasing strain. The 2008 global
financial crisis exposed deep flaws in
unregulated financial markets, resulting in a
$15 trillion loss in global wealth. Meanwhile,
economic inequality has surged: in the United
States, the top 1% of earners captured 20% of
total national income by 2020, compared to
10% in 1980, fuelling populist backlash against
neoliberal economics.
The rise of protectionism, most notably with
Brexit and the US-China trade war, has
created uncertainty in global markets. The US
imposed tariffs on $360 billion worth of
Chinese goods by 2019, marking a significant
break from the decades-long commitment to
free trade.
Finally, the economic success of China under
state-managed capitalism presents a real
alternative and challenge to free market
capitalism. With China looking to expand its
geopolitical influence through the Belt and
Road Initiative (BRI), this model may be
exported and become more common.
The Rule of Law
The rule of law continues to be a fundamental
component of governance in many parts of the
world. In countries across Europe, North
America, and Asia, judicial independence
remains strong, with legal frameworks that
ensure accountability and protect individual
rights.
Nevertheless, the rule of law has been
increasingly undermined in various parts of the
world. Democratic backsliding, especially in
countries like Hungary, Poland, and Turkey,
has weakened judicial independence as
populist governments seek to consolidate
power. Leaders in these countries have
