Democracy
Democracy remains the most common form of
governance across the world. Over 60% of
nations hold regular elections, and in many
countries, democratic institutions continue to
function effectively. In the United States,
Western Europe, Japan, and Australia,
democratic governance remains robust, with
active political participation, free press, and
protections for civil liberties.
Internationally, organisations like the United
Nations and the European Union continue to
promote democratic norms, supporting
elections, transparency, and human rights
around the globe. Even in fragile democracies,
pro-democracy movements and civil society
groups remain active, pushing for greater
freedoms and accountability.
Yet democracy is facing a serious decline in
various regions, with the rise of populism and
authoritarianism presenting significant threats.
In Hungary and Poland, populist leaders have
weakened democratic institutions, reduced
press freedom, and undermined judicial
independence, turning these countries into
illiberal democracies. Similarly, in Turkey and
Brazil, elected leaders have moved towards
authoritarianism, using democratic mandates
to consolidate power and restrict opposition.
Even in established democracies like the
United States, the rise of political polarisation
and populism, highlighted by events like the
storming of the US Capitol in 2021, has raised
concerns about the future of democratic
stability. In many regions, democratic values
are being eroded as populist and authoritarian
movements gain ground, threatening the
integrity of elections and political systems.
Further, Russia, China and Iran are
increasingly cooperating and therefore forming
a non-democratic axis that challenges the
United States and liberal international order.
